Here is the exact stack I would commit to, given what you already have (Python/FastAPI, React, OpenShift, Tachyon, mpnet embeddings in the knowledge hub, SQL Server skills on the team).

The stack

Layer	Use this	Why	Not this
Backend	Python 3.12, FastAPI, Pydantic v2, SQLAlchemy 2 + Alembic	Already your POC stack; Pydantic models double as the LLM output schemas	Django (heavier, no gain)
Frontend	React + TypeScript (Vite), TanStack Query, react-pdf (pdf.js) for citation highlights	The SMC review screen is “evidence map beside the PDF with highlights”; pdf.js does that natively	Any framework the bank has not already approved
Database	Postgres 16 + pgvector + built-in full-text search, row-level security per tenant	One database for records, chunks, vectors and the run log; tenant isolation enforced in SQL, not prompts	A separate vector DB on day one. If the bank standard is SQL Server, SQL Server 2025’s native vector type is the alternative; confirm it is available in your environment
Object storage	The bank’s S3-compatible storage (OpenShift Data Foundation or equivalent)	Originals stored once, keyed by SHA-256	Storing PDFs in the database
Document extraction	Docling (MIT) with its built-in OCR fallback	Keeps page numbers and headings, handles tables; permissive licence passes OSS review	PyMuPDF (AGPL), MinerU and marker (revenue-based licence terms)
Embeddings and reranking	sentence-transformers with all-mpnet-base-v2 (what the hub uses), bge-reranker-v2-m3, both in a CPU pod	Zero tokens for retrieval; nothing leaves the bank	Calling Tachyon for embeddings
LLM access	A thin tachyon_client plus Instructor for schema-validated retries; your own router module for tiers, escalation, budgets and cache keys	The use cases are fixed pipelines, so a 300-line router you control beats a framework you have to defend in security review	LangChain or LlamaIndex as the core; LiteLLM proxy only if Tachyon exposes OpenAI-compatible endpoints
Caching and limits	Redis (response cache, rate limits, budgets); Postgres table if Redis is not available	Exact-match cache keyed by tenant + use case + prompt version + model + input hash	Semantic cache across tenants
Batch jobs	Hatchet (Postgres-backed, retries, cron, concurrency keys); Celery if Redis already exists	Lightest option with idempotency on OpenShift	Airflow, Temporal (too heavy for this)
Observability	OpenTelemetry SDK with GenAI conventions, Langfuse self-hosted (Helm); ai_run table in Postgres as the source of truth; infra metrics to whatever the bank already runs	Prompt versions, traces and per-call cost in one place; the Admin screen reads the table	A SaaS observability vendor (data egress)
Evaluation	pytest + DeepEval, promptfoo for JSON-schema regression, golden sets in the repo, runs in CI	Blocks a prompt or model change that lowers accuracy	Manual spot checks
Guardrails	Your own code first: delimiters around document text, strict JSON, citation check, allowlist of actions; NeMo Guardrails later if needed	The citation check in code is the control auditors understand	llm-guard (archived July 2026)
Auth	Bank SSO (OIDC) for PBL/SMC/RM/Admin; separate identity for suppliers (external IdP or magic link)	Roles map to data scope in the database	Role checks in prompts
Deployment	OpenShift, three deployments: api, worker, ml (embed/rerank); Helm or Kustomize; secrets from the bank vault	The ML pod needs about 4 CPU and 16 GB RAM; the others are small	One monolith pod
Dev tooling	GitHub Copilot (you have it), Ruff, mypy, pre-commit, Playwright for end-to-end (your existing framework)	Nothing new to approve
